SOLID Principles — Complete Guide 🏦
(One Fintech Story, All 5 Principles)

The System We're Building
A user initiates a payment on Paytm → system processes it → saves to DB → sends notification → generates receipt
We'll build this system, violate every principle first, then fix it. Let's go! 👇

S — Single Responsibility Principle
The Rule
"One class = One job. A class should have only ONE reason to change."
Real World Analogy
In a bank, the teller doesn't do fraud detection, accounting, and IT support. Different departments handle each job. Same idea in code.
❌ Bad — One class doing everything
class PaymentService {
    void processPayment(String type, double amount, String userId) {

        // Payment logic
        if (type.equals("UPI")) { /* UPI logic */ }
        else if (type.equals("CREDIT_CARD")) { /* Card logic */ }

        // DB logic 🚫
        Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://...");
        conn.prepareStatement("INSERT INTO payments...").execute();

        // Notification logic 🚫
        SMSClient.send(userId, "Payment of ₹" + amount + " successful");

        // Receipt logic 🚫
        PDFGenerator.generate("receipt_" + userId + ".pdf");

        // Fraud check 🚫
        if (amount > 50000) { flagTransaction(); }
    }
}
Problem: If SMS provider changes, DB changes, PDF format changes — all lead back to this ONE class. That's 5 reasons to change = 5 SRP violations.
✅ Fix — Split into focused classes
// Each class owns exactly ONE job

class PaymentProcessor {
    void process(Payment payment) { /* only payment logic */ }
}

class PaymentRepository {
    void save(Payment payment) { /* only DB logic */ }
}

class NotificationService {
    void notify(String userId, String msg) { /* only SMS/Email */ }
}

class ReceiptGenerator {
    void generate(Payment payment) { /* only PDF receipt */ }
}

class FraudDetectionService {
    boolean isSuspicious(Payment payment) { /* only fraud logic */ }
}
Interview Line
"SRP means each class has one job. In our payment system, PaymentProcessor processes, NotificationService notifies, FraudDetectionService checks fraud — never the same class."

O — Open/Closed Principle
The Rule
"Your class should be OPEN for extension (add new things) but CLOSED for modification (don't touch existing code)."
Real World Analogy
A payment terminal at a shop doesn't need to be replaced when a new card type launches. You just add support — the terminal stays the same.
❌ Bad — Every new payment method breaks existing code
class PaymentProcessor {
    void process(String type, double amount) {
        if (type.equals("UPI")) { /* UPI logic */ }
        else if (type.equals("CREDIT_CARD")) { /* Card logic */ }
        else if (type.equals("NET_BANKING")) { /* NetBanking */ }
        // Adding BNPL = you MUST touch this class 🚫 dangerous!
    }
}
✅ Fix — Use interface, add new class for each method
// The contract — define once, never change
interface PaymentMethod {
    void pay(double amount, String userId);
}

// Each payment type is its own class
class UPIPayment implements PaymentMethod {
    public void pay(double amount, String userId) {
        System.out.println("Paid ₹" + amount + " via UPI");
    }
}

class CreditCardPayment implements PaymentMethod {
    public void pay(double amount, String userId) {
        System.out.println("Charged ₹" + amount + " on Credit Card");
    }
}

// Adding BNPL = just a NEW class, nothing existing is touched ✅
class BNPLPayment implements PaymentMethod {
    public void pay(double amount, String userId) {
        System.out.println("Buy Now Pay Later activated for ₹" + amount);
    }
}

// Processor NEVER changes no matter how many methods you add
class PaymentProcessor {
    void process(PaymentMethod method, double amount, String userId) {
        method.pay(amount, userId); // ✅ works with any payment method
    }
}
Interview Line
"OCP means adding BNPL = new class, NOT modifying PaymentProcessor. We achieve this through interfaces — extend by adding, never by modifying."

L — Liskov Substitution Principle
The Rule
"If class B extends class A, then wherever you use A, you should be able to plug in B — and everything should still work. No surprises, no exceptions."
Think of it as a promise — a child class must keep the promises made by the parent class.
Real World Analogy
A Debit Card and Credit Card are both "Cards." You can swipe both at any POS machine. If someone gave you a card that explodes on swipe — that's LSP violation! The machine trusted card-like behaviour.
Simplest Example First
class Card {
    void swipe() { System.out.println("Payment done!"); }
}

class DebitCard extends Card {
    void swipe() { System.out.println("₹500 debited!"); } // ✅ keeps the promise
}

class LockedCard extends Card {
    void swipe() { throw new Exception("Card is blocked!"); } // 🚫 breaks the machine!
}
The vending machine trusted any Card would work on swipe(). LockedCard broke that trust → LSP Violated.
❌ Bad — FD Account breaks the BankAccount promise
class BankAccount {
    double balance;
    void withdraw(double amount) {  // PROMISE: you can always withdraw
        balance -= amount;
        System.out.println("Withdrawn ₹" + amount);
    }
}

// Savings keeps the promise ✅
class SavingsAccount extends BankAccount {
    void withdraw(double amount) {
        if (balance >= amount) {
            balance -= amount;
            System.out.println("Withdrawn from Savings ₹" + amount);
        }
    }
}

// FD BREAKS the promise 🚫
class FixedDepositAccount extends BankAccount {
    void withdraw(double amount) {
        throw new Exception("Cannot withdraw from FD before maturity!"); // 🚫
    }
}
The problem:
void processWithdrawal(BankAccount account, double amount) {
    account.withdraw(amount); // trusts the promise
}

processWithdrawal(new SavingsAccount(), 500);      // ✅ works
processWithdrawal(new FixedDepositAccount(), 500); // 💥 BLOWS UP
✅ Fix — Redesign the hierarchy honestly
abstract class BankAccount {
    double balance;
    void checkBalance() { System.out.println("Balance: ₹" + balance); }
}

interface Withdrawable {
    void withdraw(double amount);
}

// Savings CAN withdraw ✅
class SavingsAccount extends BankAccount implements Withdrawable {
    public void withdraw(double amount) {
        balance -= amount;
        System.out.println("Withdrawn ₹" + amount);
    }
}

// FD CANNOT withdraw — and we don't pretend it can ✅
class FixedDepositAccount extends BankAccount {
    void redeemOnMaturity() {
        System.out.println("FD matured! Credited to your account.");
    }
}

// Now this is 100% safe — only Withdrawable accounts allowed
void processWithdrawal(Withdrawable account, double amount) {
    account.withdraw(amount); // ✅ no surprises ever
}
🧠 Mental Test for LSP
Ask yourself: "Can I replace the parent with the child everywhere, and will the program still work correctly?"
* SavingsAccount replaces BankAccount → ✅ Yes
* FixedDepositAccount replaces BankAccount → ❌ No, throws exception → Fix the hierarchy!
Interview Line
"LSP means child classes must honour the contract of the parent. FD Account can't honour withdraw() so it shouldn't extend BankAccount directly. Instead we use a Withdrawable interface only for accounts that actually support it."

I — Interface Segregation Principle
The Rule
"Don't force a class to implement methods it will never use."
Real World Analogy
A Chef being asked to fix the plumbing. A Developer being asked to call customers. That's ISP violation — your role shouldn't include things you'll never do!
Simplest Example First — Paytm Employee Roles
// ❌ FAT interface — one size fits all
interface PaytmEmployee {
    void writeCode();      // only developers
    void designUI();       // only designers
    void callCustomers();  // only support
}

// Developer is FORCED to implement things they don't do 🚫
class Developer implements PaytmEmployee {
    public void writeCode() { System.out.println("Writing APIs!"); } // ✅
    public void designUI() { ??? }        // 🚫 I'm a dev, not a designer!
    public void callCustomers() { ??? }   // 🚫 I'm a dev, not support!
}
✅ Fix — Split the interface
interface Developer    { void writeCode(); }
interface UIDesigner   { void designUI(); }
interface SupportAgent { void callCustomers(); }

class BackendDev implements Developer {
    public void writeCode() { System.out.println("Writing APIs!"); } // ✅ only this
}

class Designer implements UIDesigner {
    public void designUI() { System.out.println("Designing screens!"); } // ✅ only this
}
Now the Fintech Example — Payment Methods
// ❌ FAT interface forcing everything on everyone
interface Payment {
    void pay();
    void refund();          // Prepaid card can't refund
    void convertToEMI();    // UPI doesn't have EMI
    void splitBill();       // Credit card can't split
}

// Prepaid Card forced to implement things it can't do 🚫
class PrepaidCard implements Payment {
    public void pay() { System.out.println("Paid via Prepaid Card"); }
    public void refund() { throw new Exception("Can't refund!"); }       // 🚫
    public void convertToEMI() { throw new Exception("No EMI!"); }       // 🚫
    public void splitBill() { throw new Exception("Can't split!"); }     // 🚫
}
✅ Fix — Small focused interfaces
interface Payable       { void pay(); }
interface Refundable    { void refund(); }
interface EMICapable    { void convertToEMI(); }
interface BillSplitter  { void splitBill(); }

// UPI — pay, refund, split. NO EMI.
class UPIPayment implements Payable, Refundable, BillSplitter {
    public void pay()       { System.out.println("Paid via UPI ✅"); }
    public void refund()    { System.out.println("Refunded via UPI ✅"); }
    public void splitBill() { System.out.println("Bill split via UPI ✅"); }
}

// Credit Card — pay, refund, EMI. NO split.
class CreditCard implements Payable, Refundable, EMICapable {
    public void pay()          { System.out.println("Paid via Credit Card ✅"); }
    public void refund()       { System.out.println("Refunded ✅"); }
    public void convertToEMI() { System.out.println("Converted to 6 month EMI ✅"); }
}

// Prepaid Card — ONLY pay. Nothing else.
class PrepaidCard implements Payable {
    public void pay() { System.out.println("Paid via Prepaid Card ✅"); }
}
🧠 Mental Test for ISP
"Is any class writing throw new Exception() or leaving a method blank?" If yes → ISP violated → split the interface.
Interview Line
"ISP says don't create fat interfaces. UPI doesn't support EMI, Prepaid Card doesn't support refund — so instead of one big Payment interface, we split into Payable, Refundable, EMICapable. Each class implements only what it actually supports."

D — Dependency Inversion Principle
Understanding the Name First
"Dependency" = When Class A needs Class B to work → A depends on B.
class PaymentService {
    RazorpayGateway gateway = new RazorpayGateway(); // PaymentService DEPENDS on Razorpay
}
"Inversion" = Flip / Reverse the relationship.
// Normal (bad)
PaymentService  ──directly uses──►  RazorpayGateway

// Inverted (good) — both point to the interface
PaymentService  ──►  <<Interface>>  ◄──  RazorpayGateway
The Rule
"High-level classes should not depend on low-level classes. Both should depend on an abstraction (interface)."
Real World Analogy — The Shop & Job Description
You own a shop (PaymentService). You hire Ramesh (RazorpayGateway) directly.
Shop ──── directly depends on ────► Ramesh
Problem: Ramesh goes on leave → your shop stops working!
Solution: Write a Job Description (Interface) instead:
"Payment Handler must know how to:
 - collect payment
 - give receipt"
Now anyone who fits the job description works — Ramesh, Suresh, or PayU-wala Dinesh.
Shop ──► Job Description ◄── Ramesh
              ◄──────────── Suresh
              ◄──────────── Dinesh
Your shop never changes. Only the person changes. That's DIP!
❌ Bad — PaymentService married to Razorpay
class RazorpayGateway {
    void makePayment(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paying via Razorpay ₹" + amount);
    }
}

class PaymentService {
    RazorpayGateway gateway = new RazorpayGateway(); // 🚫 directly depends on Razorpay

    void pay(double amount) {
        gateway.makePayment(amount);
    }
}
// Switching to PayU = rewriting PaymentService 🚫 dangerous!
✅ Fix — Depend on interface, inject from outside
Step 1 — Create the Job Description (Interface)
interface PaymentGateway {
    void makePayment(double amount);
}
Step 2 — Anyone who fits the role implements it
class RazorpayGateway implements PaymentGateway {
    public void makePayment(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paying via Razorpay ₹" + amount); // ✅
    }
}

class PayUGateway implements PaymentGateway {
    public void makePayment(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paying via PayU ₹" + amount); // ✅
    }
}

class StripeGateway implements PaymentGateway { // new gateway — nothing else changes ✅
    public void makePayment(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Paying via Stripe ₹" + amount);
    }
}
Step 3 — PaymentService only knows the interface
class PaymentService {
    PaymentGateway gateway; // 👈 interface, NOT RazorpayGateway

    // Gateway injected from outside — this is called Dependency Injection
    PaymentService(PaymentGateway gateway) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
    }

    void pay(double amount) {
        gateway.makePayment(amount); // works with ANY gateway ✅
    }
}
Step 4 — Swap freely, zero changes inside PaymentService
PaymentService s1 = new PaymentService(new RazorpayGateway());
s1.pay(500); // "Paying via Razorpay ₹500"

PaymentService s2 = new PaymentService(new PayUGateway()); // ✅ zero change in PaymentService
s2.pay(500); // "Paying via PayU ₹500"
DIP vs DI — Interviewers Love This!
Term	What it means
DIP	The principle — depend on abstractions, not concrete classes
DI (Dependency Injection)	The technique — pass the dependency from outside
Spring IoC	The framework that does DI automatically for you
Interview Line
"DIP means PaymentService should not hardcode RazorpayGateway. Instead both depend on PaymentGateway interface. Switching from Razorpay to PayU requires zero changes in PaymentService — you just inject a different gateway. This is exactly what Spring does with @Autowired."

