Dependency Injection in Spring Boot
Complete Notes — Interview Ready 🎯

What is Dependency Injection (DI)?
"Instead of a class creating its own dependencies, someone else creates and provides them."
The "someone else" in Spring Boot = Spring IoC Container (Application Context).

The Problem — Without DI
Scenario: PaymentService needs FraudDetectionService
// ❌ WITHOUT DI — class creates its own dependencies

public class PaymentService {

    // Directly creating dependency inside the class
    private FraudDetectionService fraudService = new FraudDetectionService();
    private NotificationService notificationService = new NotificationService();
    private PaymentRepository repository = new PaymentRepository();

    public void processPayment(String userId, double amount) {
        fraudService.check(userId, amount);
        repository.save(userId, amount);
        notificationService.notify(userId);
    }
}
What Goes Wrong? — 5 Real Problems

Problem 1: Tight Coupling 🔗
// PaymentService is MARRIED to FraudDetectionService
private FraudDetectionService fraudService = new FraudDetectionService();

// Tomorrow your team says: "Use MLFraudDetectionService instead"
// You have to OPEN PaymentService and change it 🚫
// What if PaymentService is used in 50 other places?
Result: One change = modify many files. High risk of bugs.

Problem 2: Cannot Test Properly 🧪
// ❌ Without DI — you CANNOT mock FraudDetectionService in unit tests

public class PaymentService {
    private FraudDetectionService fraudService = new FraudDetectionService();
    // This always creates a REAL FraudDetectionService
    // Real service might call actual DB, actual APIs
    // Your unit test becomes an integration test 🚫
}

// Unit test becomes impossible to isolate
@Test
void testPayment() {
    PaymentService service = new PaymentService();
    // FraudDetectionService will hit REAL database 🚫
    // Test is slow, unreliable, environment-dependent
}
Result: Unit tests become impossible. Every test hits real DB/APIs.

Problem 3: Object Created Every Time (Memory Waste) 💾
// ❌ Every time PaymentService is created, it creates 3 new objects

PaymentService s1 = new PaymentService();
// Creates: new FraudDetectionService + new NotificationService + new PaymentRepository

PaymentService s2 = new PaymentService();
// Creates: ANOTHER FraudDetectionService + ANOTHER NotificationService + ANOTHER PaymentRepository

// In a real app with 1000 requests/sec = 3000 unnecessary objects/sec 🚫
Result: Memory leaks, performance issues, connection pool exhaustion.

Problem 4: Cascading Constructor Nightmare 😱
// What if FraudDetectionService itself needs dependencies?

public class FraudDetectionService {
    private MLModelService mlModel = new MLModelService();
    private RiskScoreRepository riskRepo = new RiskScoreRepository();
}

// And MLModelService needs dependencies?
public class MLModelService {
    private DatabaseConnection db = new DatabaseConnection();
    private CacheService cache = new CacheService();
}

// Now PaymentService indirectly creates 6+ objects
// You have NO control, NO visibility into this chain 🚫
Result: Uncontrollable object creation chains. Hard to debug.

Problem 5: Cannot Swap Implementations 🔄
// ❌ What if you want different fraud services for different environments?
// DEV  → MockFraudService (fast, no real calls)
// PROD → RealFraudService (actual ML model)

public class PaymentService {
    // Hardcoded — same in DEV and PROD 🚫
    private FraudDetectionService fraudService = new FraudDetectionService();
}
Result: Same implementation in all environments. Cannot configure per environment.

The Solution — With Dependency Injection ✅
// ✅ WITH DI — Spring creates and injects dependencies

@Service
public class PaymentService {

    // Just DECLARE what you need — don't create it
    private final FraudDetectionService fraudService;
    private final NotificationService notificationService;
    private final PaymentRepository repository;

    // Spring PROVIDES them through constructor
    @Autowired
    public PaymentService(FraudDetectionService fraudService,
                          NotificationService notificationService,
                          PaymentRepository repository) {
        this.fraudService = fraudService;
        this.notificationService = notificationService;
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    public void processPayment(String userId, double amount) {
        fraudService.check(userId, amount);
        repository.save(userId, amount);
        notificationService.notify(userId);
    }
}

How Spring DI Fixes All 5 Problems
Fix 1: Loose Coupling ✅
// Use interface, not concrete class
private final FraudDetectionService fraudService; // interface

// Tomorrow switch to ML version?
// Just change ONE config — PaymentService untouched ✅
@Bean
public FraudDetectionService fraudDetectionService() {
    return new MLFraudDetectionService(); // swap here, nowhere else
}

Fix 2: Easy Unit Testing ✅
// With DI — you can INJECT a mock in tests
@Test
void testPayment() {
    // Create a fake fraud service — no DB, no APIs
    FraudDetectionService mockFraud = Mockito.mock(FraudDetectionService.class);
    NotificationService mockNotif = Mockito.mock(NotificationService.class);
    PaymentRepository mockRepo = Mockito.mock(PaymentRepository.class);

    // Inject mocks into PaymentService
    PaymentService service = new PaymentService(mockFraud, mockNotif, mockRepo);

    // Now test purely — no real DB, no real APIs ✅
    service.processPayment("user123", 500);

    Mockito.verify(mockFraud).check("user123", 500); // verify mock was called
}

Fix 3: Singleton — One Object, Shared Everywhere ✅
// Spring creates ONE instance of each @Service and reuses it
@Service // Singleton by default
public class FraudDetectionService { }

// Request 1 → Spring injects SAME FraudDetectionService instance
// Request 2 → Spring injects SAME FraudDetectionService instance
// Request 1000 → Spring injects SAME FraudDetectionService instance

// Result: No memory waste, connection pools work correctly ✅

Fix 4: Spring Manages the Entire Object Graph ✅
// Spring figures out the entire dependency chain automatically

// You just declare:
@Service public class PaymentService {
    PaymentService(FraudDetectionService f) { }
}

@Service public class FraudDetectionService {
    FraudDetectionService(MLModelService m, RiskScoreRepository r) { }
}

@Service public class MLModelService {
    MLModelService(DatabaseConnection db, CacheService c) { }
}

// Spring auto-creates in correct order:
// 1. DatabaseConnection
// 2. CacheService
// 3. MLModelService (needs 1 & 2)
// 4. RiskScoreRepository
// 5. FraudDetectionService (needs 3 & 4)
// 6. PaymentService (needs 5) ✅

Fix 5: Swap Implementations Per Environment ✅
// application-dev.properties
fraud.service=mock

// application-prod.properties
fraud.service=real

// Config class
@Configuration
public class FraudConfig {

    @Value("${fraud.service}")
    private String fraudServiceType;

    @Bean
    public FraudDetectionService fraudDetectionService() {
        if (fraudServiceType.equals("mock")) {
            return new MockFraudDetectionService(); // DEV
        }
        return new RealFraudDetectionService(); // PROD ✅
    }
}

The Strategy Pattern + DI Example (Full Picture)
// Interface — the contract
public interface PaymentStrategy {
    void pay(double amount, String userId);
}

// Implementations — Spring registers each as a bean
@Component("upi")
public class UPIPaymentStrategy implements PaymentStrategy {
    public void pay(double amount, String userId) {
        System.out.println("UPI: ₹" + amount + " paid by " + userId);
    }
}

@Component("card")
public class CardPaymentStrategy implements PaymentStrategy {
    public void pay(double amount, String userId) {
        System.out.println("Card: ₹" + amount + " charged to " + userId);
    }
}

@Component("wallet")
public class WalletPaymentStrategy implements PaymentStrategy {
    public void pay(double amount, String userId) {
        System.out.println("Wallet: ₹" + amount + " debited from " + userId);
    }
}

// PaymentService — Spring injects ALL strategies automatically
@Service
public class PaymentService {

    private final Map<String, PaymentStrategy> strategies;

    @Autowired
    public PaymentService(Map<String, PaymentStrategy> strategies) {
        // Spring builds this map automatically:
        // Step 1: Scans all @Component classes
        // Step 2: Finds all that implement PaymentStrategy
        // Step 3: Creates map → { "upi" → UPI bean, "card" → Card bean, "wallet" → Wallet bean }
        // Step 4: Injects this map into PaymentService constructor
        this.strategies = strategies;
    }

    public void processPayment(String type, double amount, String userId) {
        PaymentStrategy strategy = strategies.get(type);
        if (strategy == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown payment type: " + type);
        }
        strategy.pay(amount, userId);
    }
}
What Happens Inside Spring Step by Step
App Startup
    │
    ▼
Spring scans com.one97.* (your @ComponentScan)
    │
    ├── Finds @Component("upi")    → creates UPIPaymentStrategy bean
    ├── Finds @Component("card")   → creates CardPaymentStrategy bean
    ├── Finds @Component("wallet") → creates WalletPaymentStrategy bean
    └── Finds @Service             → needs to create PaymentService bean
              │
              ▼
         Constructor needs Map<String, PaymentStrategy>
              │
              ▼
         Spring looks in Application Context:
         "Who implements PaymentStrategy?"
              │
              ├── UPIPaymentStrategy (name="upi")    ✅
              ├── CardPaymentStrategy (name="card")  ✅
              └── WalletPaymentStrategy (name="wallet") ✅
              │
              ▼
         Builds Map:
         { "upi" → UPI bean, "card" → Card bean, "wallet" → Wallet bean }
              │
              ▼
         Injects into PaymentService constructor ✅
              │
              ▼
         PaymentService bean is ready!

3 Types of Dependency Injection in Spring
1. Constructor Injection (RECOMMENDED ✅)
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final FraudDetectionService fraudService;

    @Autowired // optional in newer Spring versions
    public PaymentService(FraudDetectionService fraudService) {
        this.fraudService = fraudService;
    }
}
Why best:
* Dependencies are final — immutable, thread-safe
* Fail-fast — app won't start if dependency is missing
* Easy to unit test — just pass mock in constructor

2. Field Injection (Common but NOT recommended)
@Service
public class PaymentService {

    @Autowired // Spring injects directly into field via reflection
    private FraudDetectionService fraudService;
}
Why avoid:
* Cannot make field final
* Hard to unit test — can't inject mock without Spring
* Hides dependencies — not obvious what class needs

3. Setter Injection (For optional dependencies)
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private FraudDetectionService fraudService;

    @Autowired(required = false) // optional dependency
    public void setFraudService(FraudDetectionService fraudService) {
        this.fraudService = fraudService;
    }
}
When to use: Only for optional dependencies that have a default fallback.

DI vs IoC — Know the Difference
Term	Full Form	Meaning
DI	Dependency Injection	How dependencies are provided (constructor/field/setter)
IoC	Inversion of Control	You don't control object creation — Spring does
IoC Container	—	Spring's ApplicationContext that manages all beans
DI is how IoC is implemented in Spring.

Quick Cheat Sheet
Without DI	With DI (Spring)
new FraudService() inside class	Spring injects FraudService
Tight coupling	Loose coupling via interfaces
Hard to unit test	Easy to mock and test
New object every time	Singleton — one shared instance
Cannot swap implementations	Swap via config/properties
Manual dependency chain	Spring manages entire graph
Interview One-Liner
"Without DI, classes create their own dependencies using new — this causes tight coupling, makes unit testing impossible, wastes memory, and makes swapping implementations hard. Spring's DI inverts this control — Spring creates and injects dependencies. This gives us loose coupling via interfaces, easy mocking in tests, singleton beans for memory efficiency, and the ability to swap implementations via config. In our Paytm payment system, PaymentService doesn't create FraudDetectionService — Spring injects it. This means in tests, we inject a mock; in prod, Spring injects the real one."

Singleton vs Prototype — Complete Notes

The Only Question to Ask
Does this class store USER-SPECIFIC data in CLASS-LEVEL FIELDS?

         YES → @Scope("prototype")
         NO  → Singleton (default) ✅

PART 1 — What is Singleton?
One object created ONCE, shared by EVERYONE, lives for entire app lifetime.
@Service  // Singleton by default — Spring creates ONE instance ✅
public class PaymentService {
    // No user-specific fields
    // Just repos and logic
}
App starts → Spring creates ONE PaymentService object → stored in memory forever

Request 1 (Anubhav) ──────┐
Request 2 (Rahul)   ───────┼──► [ Same PaymentService Object ]
Request 3 (Vikram)  ──────┘
Safe because: PaymentService has NO user-specific fields. It just has tools (repos) and logic.

PART 2 — What is Prototype?
New object created for EVERY request/injection. Each user gets their OWN copy.
@Service
@Scope("prototype") // Fresh object every time
public class PaymentReceiptGenerator {
    private String userId;   // user-specific field
    private double amount;   // user-specific field
}
Request 1 (Anubhav) ──► [ PaymentReceiptGenerator A ]  userId="Anubhav", amount=500
Request 2 (Rahul)   ──► [ PaymentReceiptGenerator B ]  userId="Rahul",   amount=10000
Request 3 (Vikram)  ──► [ PaymentReceiptGenerator C ]  userId="Vikram",  amount=2000

3 requests = 3 separate objects = zero conflict ✅

