Design Patterns — Complete Guide 🚀
Correct Sequence: Creational → Structural → Behavioral

🏗️ CREATIONAL PATTERNS
How objects are CREATED

1️⃣ Singleton Pattern
Rule
"Only ONE instance of a class exists in the entire application."
Simple Analogy
Your company has one database connection pool. Every request uses the same pool — you don't create a new pool for every request. That would be insane.
❌ Bad — New object every time
public class PaymentService {
    public void pay(double amount) {
        // Creates new DB connection every time 🚫
        DatabaseConnection conn = new DatabaseConnection();
        conn.execute("INSERT INTO payments...");
    }
}
// 1000 requests = 1000 DB connections = app crashes 🚫
✅ Good — Pure Java Singleton
public class DatabaseConnection {
    private static DatabaseConnection instance;

    private DatabaseConnection() {
        System.out.println("DB Connected!"); // runs ONCE
    }

    public static synchronized DatabaseConnection getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new DatabaseConnection();
        }
        return instance; // always returns SAME object
    }
}

// Proof
DatabaseConnection c1 = DatabaseConnection.getInstance();
DatabaseConnection c2 = DatabaseConnection.getInstance();
System.out.println(c1 == c2); // true ✅ same object
🌱 Spring Boot — Singleton is DEFAULT
@Service     // ONE instance → Singleton ✅
public class PaymentService { }

@Repository  // ONE instance → Singleton ✅
public class PaymentRepository { }

@Component   // ONE instance → Singleton ✅
public class FraudDetectionService { }
Interview Line
"Spring creates every @Service, @Repository, @Component as a Singleton by default. One instance shared across all requests — saves memory, improves performance."

2️⃣ Factory Pattern
Rule
"Let a Factory decide WHICH object to create. Don't use new directly in business logic."
Simple Analogy
You don't build your own Debit Card. You tell the bank — "I want a Platinum Card" — the Card Factory creates it. You don't care how it's made.
❌ Bad — Business logic decides creation
public class PaymentService {
    public void pay(String type, double amount) {
        PaymentMethod method;

        // Business logic polluted with creation logic 🚫
        if (type.equals("UPI"))  method = new UPIPayment();
        else if (type.equals("CARD")) method = new CardPayment();
        else if (type.equals("WALLET")) method = new WalletPayment();
        // Adding BNPL = touch this class 🚫

        method.pay(amount);
    }
}
✅ Good — Factory handles creation
interface PaymentMethod { void pay(double amount); }

class UPIPayment    implements PaymentMethod {
    public void pay(double amount) { System.out.println("UPI ₹" + amount); }
}
class CardPayment   implements PaymentMethod {
    public void pay(double amount) { System.out.println("Card ₹" + amount); }
}
class WalletPayment implements PaymentMethod {
    public void pay(double amount) { System.out.println("Wallet ₹" + amount); }
}

// Factory — ONE place responsible for creation
public class PaymentMethodFactory {
    public static PaymentMethod create(String type) {
        switch (type) {
            case "UPI":    return new UPIPayment();
            case "CARD":   return new CardPayment();
            case "WALLET": return new WalletPayment();
            default: throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown: " + type);
        }
    }
}

// Business logic is CLEAN — no new, no if-else
public class PaymentService {
    public void pay(String type, double amount) {
        PaymentMethod method = PaymentMethodFactory.create(type); // ✅
        method.pay(amount);
    }
}
🌱 Spring Boot Version
@Configuration
public class PaymentConfig {
    @Bean // This is a Factory! ✅
    public Map<String, PaymentMethod> paymentFactory() {
        Map<String, PaymentMethod> factory = new HashMap<>();
        factory.put("UPI",    new UPIPayment());
        factory.put("CARD",   new CardPayment());
        factory.put("WALLET", new WalletPayment());
        return factory;
    }
}
Interview Line
"@Configuration with @Bean methods is Factory Pattern in Spring. The config class decides what to create and how — business logic just asks for the object."

3️⃣ Builder Pattern
Rule
"Build complex objects step by step. No more giant constructors."
Simple Analogy
Filling a Loan Application — you fill it step by step: personal details → income → collateral → guarantor. You don't dump everything in at once.
❌ Bad — Too many constructor params
// What is true? What is 50000? NO IDEA without docs 🚫
Transaction t = new Transaction(
    "TXN001", "user123", 50000, "UPI", "SUCCESS", true, false, "INR"
);
✅ Good — Builder Pattern
