🔗 STRUCTURAL PATTERNS
How objects are CONNECTED and WRAPPED

4️⃣ Proxy Pattern
Rule
"A Proxy sits in front of an object and controls access to it — adds extra behaviour without changing the original."
Simple Analogy
A bank manager's secretary. You don't go directly to the manager. The secretary first checks: "Do you have an appointment? Are you authorized?" — then lets you in. The manager doesn't change. The secretary adds the checking.
Without Proxy:   You ──────────────────────────► Manager
With Proxy:      You ──► Secretary (checks) ────► Manager
❌ Bad — Direct call, no control
public class PaymentService {
    public void processPayment(double amount) {
        // No logging, no security check, no caching 🚫
        System.out.println("Payment of ₹" + amount + " processed");
    }
}
✅ Good — Proxy adds behaviour
// Real object — pure business logic
public class PaymentService {
    public void processPayment(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Payment of ₹" + amount + " processed");
    }
}

// Proxy — wraps PaymentService, adds extra behaviour
public class PaymentServiceProxy {
    private PaymentService realService = new PaymentService();

    public void processPayment(double amount) {

        // BEFORE — added by proxy
        System.out.println("LOG: Payment started for ₹" + amount);
        System.out.println("SECURITY: Checking fraud...");

        // Call the REAL object
        realService.processPayment(amount);

        // AFTER — added by proxy
        System.out.println("LOG: Payment completed");
        System.out.println("AUDIT: Transaction recorded");
    }
}
// PaymentService has NO idea it's being proxied ✅
// PaymentService stays clean ✅
🌱 Spring Boot — AOP IS Proxy Pattern!
Spring uses Proxy automatically for @Transactional, @Cacheable, @Async!
@Service
public class PaymentService {

    @Transactional  // ← Spring creates a PROXY around this method
    public void processPayment(String userId, double amount) {
        // Your pure business logic — no transaction code here
        paymentRepository.save(userId, amount);
        loyaltyRepository.addPoints(userId, amount);
        // If anything fails → Spring PROXY auto-rolls back ✅
    }

    @Cacheable(value = "paymentCache", key = "#userId")  // ← Proxy adds caching
    public Payment getLastPayment(String userId) {
        // Spring PROXY checks cache first
        // If found → returns from cache, never calls this method
        // If not found → calls this, stores result in cache ✅
        return paymentRepository.findLatestByUserId(userId);
    }
}
What Spring Actually Does Behind the Scenes
You call: paymentService.processPayment("user123", 500)

What actually runs:
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│           Spring's Proxy (auto-generated)           │
│  1. BEGIN TRANSACTION                               │
│  2. Call real PaymentService.processPayment()  ──┐  │
│                                                  │  │
│  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┘  │
│  │   Real PaymentService                            │
│  │   - save payment to DB                           │
│  │   - add loyalty points                           │
│  └──────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │
│                                                  │   │
│  3. If success → COMMIT TRANSACTION              │   │
│  4. If error   → ROLLBACK TRANSACTION            │   │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Your PaymentService has ZERO transaction code ✅
Spring Proxy handles it all ✅
Interview Line
"@Transactional in Spring is Proxy Pattern. Spring creates a proxy around your service — adds begin/commit/rollback automatically. Your business logic stays clean. Same with @Cacheable — proxy checks cache before hitting DB."

5️⃣ Decorator Pattern
Rule
"Add new features to an object dynamically — without changing the original class or using inheritance."
Simple Analogy
A plain coffee costs ₹50. Add milk → ₹60. Add sugar → ₹65. Add caramel → ₹80. You're decorating the coffee step by step. The base coffee never changes.
Base:  Coffee (₹50)
+Milk: MilkDecorator wraps Coffee (₹60)
+Sugar: SugarDecorator wraps MilkDecorator (₹65)
Fintech Example — Payment Notifications
// Base interface
interface PaymentNotification {
    void send(String userId, double amount);
}

// Base implementation — just SMS
class SMSNotification implements PaymentNotification {
    public void send(String userId, double amount) {
        System.out.println("SMS: ₹" + amount + " paid → " + userId);
    }
}

// Decorator base
abstract class NotificationDecorator implements PaymentNotification {
    protected PaymentNotification wrapped;
    NotificationDecorator(PaymentNotification notification) {
        this.wrapped = notification;
    }
}

// Add Email on top of SMS
class EmailDecorator extends NotificationDecorator {
    EmailDecorator(PaymentNotification notification) { super(notification); }

    public void send(String userId, double amount) {
        wrapped.send(userId, amount);   // call original
        System.out.println("EMAIL: Receipt sent to " + userId); // add email
    }
}

// Add WhatsApp on top of Email+SMS
class WhatsAppDecorator extends NotificationDecorator {
    WhatsAppDecorator(PaymentNotification notification) { super(notification); }

    public void send(String userId, double amount) {
        wrapped.send(userId, amount);  // call original (which calls SMS + Email)
        System.out.println("WHATSAPP: Payment alert sent → " + userId); // add WhatsApp
    }
}

// Usage — compose any combination ✅
PaymentNotification smsOnly = new SMSNotification();
smsOnly.send("user123", 500);
// Output: SMS: ₹500 paid → user123

PaymentNotification smsAndEmail = new EmailDecorator(new SMSNotification());
smsAndEmail.send("user123", 500);
// Output: SMS: ₹500 paid → user123
//         EMAIL: Receipt sent to user123

PaymentNotification allChannels = new WhatsAppDecorator(
                                      new EmailDecorator(
                                          new SMSNotification()));
allChannels.send("user123", 500);
// Output: SMS + EMAIL + WHATSAPP ✅
Proxy vs Decorator — Key Difference
PROXY:      Controls ACCESS to the object
            (security check, caching, logging, transactions)
            Proxy knows what the real object is

DECORATOR:  Adds NEW FEATURES to the object
            (extra notifications, extra processing)
            Stacks features on top of each other

Real world:
PROXY     = Bank security guard (controls who enters)
DECORATOR = Customizing your coffee (adds features)
Interview Line
"Decorator adds features dynamically without changing the original class. In our notification system, SMSNotification is the base — we wrap it with EmailDecorator, then WhatsAppDecorator. Each adds its own behaviour and calls the wrapped object. No inheritance needed, fully composable."

6️⃣ Adapter Pattern
Rule
"Make two incompatible interfaces work together. Acts as a translator."
Simple Analogy
Your laptop charger is Type-C but the hotel room has a 3-pin socket. You use a travel adapter. The charger and socket don't change — the adapter translates between them.
Fintech Example — Old Bank API + New System
// OLD bank API — you cannot change this (third party)
public class OldBankPaymentAPI {
    public boolean makeTransfer(String fromAcc, String toAcc, int amountInPaise) {
        System.out.println("Old API: Transfer ₹" + (amountInPaise/100));
        return true;
    }
}

// YOUR system expects this interface
public interface ModernPaymentGateway {
    boolean processPayment(String userId, double amountInRupees);
}

// ADAPTER — translates between old API and new interface
public class BankAPIAdapter implements ModernPaymentGateway {

    private OldBankPaymentAPI oldAPI = new OldBankPaymentAPI(); // wraps old API

    public boolean processPayment(String userId, double amountInRupees) {
        // Translate: rupees → paise, userId → account number
        String fromAccount = getAccountNumber(userId);
        String toAccount   = "PAYTM_MERCHANT_ACC";
        int amountInPaise  = (int)(amountInRupees * 100); // rupees to paise ✅

        return oldAPI.makeTransfer(fromAccount, toAccount, amountInPaise);
    }

    private String getAccountNumber(String userId) {
        return "ACC_" + userId; // lookup logic
    }
}

// Your PaymentService uses ModernPaymentGateway — doesn't know about old API ✅
public class PaymentService {
    private ModernPaymentGateway gateway;

    PaymentService(ModernPaymentGateway gateway) {
        this.gateway = gateway;
    }

    public void pay(String userId, double amount) {
        gateway.processPayment(userId, amount); // clean ✅
    }
}

// Wire them together
PaymentService service = new PaymentService(new BankAPIAdapter()); // ✅
service.pay("user123", 500.0);
Proxy vs Adapter vs Decorator — Quick Comparison
PROXY:     Same interface, controls access
           [ You ] ──► [ Proxy ] ──► [ Real Object ]
           Same type, added behaviour

ADAPTER:   Different interfaces, translates
           [ New System ] ──► [ Adapter ] ──► [ Old System ]
           Bridges incompatible interfaces

DECORATOR: Same interface, adds features
           [ Base ] ──► [ Decorator1 ] ──► [ Decorator2 ]
           Stacks extra features
Interview Line
"Adapter Pattern when integrating with third-party or legacy systems. Our BankAPIAdapter wraps the old bank API — translates rupees to paise, userId to account number. Our PaymentService only sees ModernPaymentGateway and never knows about the old API."

🔄 BEHAVIORAL PATTERNS
How objects COMMUNICATE with each other

7️⃣ Strategy Pattern
Rule
"Define a family of algorithms, put each in a separate class, swap them at runtime."
Simple Analogy
Google Maps — "Car, Bike, or Walking?" Route strategy changes. Map app stays the same.
🌱 Spring Boot
interface PaymentStrategy { void pay(double amount, String userId); }

@Component("upi")
class UPIPaymentStrategy implements PaymentStrategy {
    public void pay(double amount, String userId) {
        System.out.println("UPI: ₹" + amount);
    }
}

@Component("card")
class CardPaymentStrategy implements PaymentStrategy {
    public void pay(double amount, String userId) {
        System.out.println("Card: ₹" + amount);
    }
}

@Service
public class PaymentService {
    private final Map<String, PaymentStrategy> strategies;

    @Autowired
    public PaymentService(Map<String, PaymentStrategy> strategies) {
        // Spring auto-builds: {"upi" → UPI bean, "card" → Card bean}
        this.strategies = strategies;
    }

    public void pay(String type, double amount, String userId) {
        strategies.get(type).pay(amount, userId); // ✅
    }
}
// Add BNPL = just new @Component("bnpl") — NOTHING else changes ✅
Interview Line
"Strategy Pattern via Spring's Map injection. Each strategy is a @Component. Spring auto-injects all implementations. Adding new payment type = new class only."

8️⃣ Observer Pattern
Rule
"When one object changes state, all dependents are notified automatically."
Simple Analogy
After Paytm payment succeeds → SMS fires, Email fires, Loyalty points fire — all automatically. Payment doesn't call each one manually.
🌱 Spring Boot — ApplicationEvent
// Event
public class PaymentSuccessEvent extends ApplicationEvent {
    private final Payment payment;
    public PaymentSuccessEvent(Object source, Payment payment) {
        super(source);
        this.payment = payment;
    }
    public Payment getPayment() { return payment; }
}

// Publisher — fires event, doesn't care who listens
@Service
public class PaymentService {
    @Autowired
    private ApplicationEventPublisher eventPublisher;

    public void processPayment(Payment payment) {
        // process...
        eventPublisher.publishEvent(
            new PaymentSuccessEvent(this, payment)
        ); // fire and forget ✅
    }
}

// Listeners react automatically
@Component
public class SMSListener {
    @EventListener
    public void onPayment(PaymentSuccessEvent e) {
        System.out.println("SMS sent to " + e.getPayment().getUserId());
    }
}

@Component
public class LoyaltyListener {
    @EventListener
    @Async // runs in separate thread ✅
    public void onPayment(PaymentSuccessEvent e) {
        System.out.println("Loyalty points added!");
    }
}
// Add WhatsApp = new @Component. PaymentService untouched ✅
Interview Line
"After payment, PaymentService fires PaymentSuccessEvent via ApplicationEventPublisher. Each listener — SMS, Loyalty, Email — reacts independently with @EventListener. OCP in action."

9️⃣ Template Method Pattern
Rule
"Define the fixed skeleton of an algorithm in base class. Let subclasses fill in the variable steps."
Simple Analogy
Every loan follows the same flow: KYC → Credit Score → Interest Rate → EMI → Approval. Only the interest rate differs per loan type.
// Base class — fixed skeleton
public abstract class LoanProcessor {

    public final void processLoan(String userId, double amount) { // FIXED
        verifyKYC(userId);                      // fixed
        checkCreditScore(userId);               // fixed
        double rate = getInterestRate();        // ← subclass fills this
        calculateEMI(amount, rate);             // fixed
        approveLoan(userId);                    // fixed
    }

    private void verifyKYC(String u) { System.out.println("KYC done"); }
    private void checkCreditScore(String u) { System.out.println("Score checked"); }
    private void calculateEMI(double a, double r) { System.out.println("EMI = ₹" + a*r/12); }
    private void approveLoan(String u) { System.out.println("Approved!"); }

    protected abstract double getInterestRate(); // subclass decides
}

@Component
class HomeLoanProcessor extends LoanProcessor {
    protected double getInterestRate() { return 0.085; } // 8.5%
}

@Component
class PersonalLoanProcessor extends LoanProcessor {
    protected double getInterestRate() { return 0.14; }  // 14%
}
🌱 Spring Boot — JdbcTemplate IS Template Method!
@Repository
public class PaymentRepository {
    @Autowired
    private JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate; // Template Method in action!

    public void save(Payment p) {
        // You only write SQL ← your variable step
        // JdbcTemplate handles: open connection, create statement,
        //                       handle exceptions, close connection ← fixed steps
        jdbcTemplate.update(
            "INSERT INTO payments(id, user_id, amount) VALUES(?,?,?)",
            p.getId(), p.getUserId(), p.getAmount()
        );
    }
}
// RestTemplate, KafkaTemplate, RedisTemplate — ALL Template Method Pattern!
Interview Line
"Template Method defines fixed algorithm with variable steps. Spring's JdbcTemplate is the perfect example — it handles connection management, you just provide the SQL query."

🔟 Chain of Responsibility Pattern
Rule
"Pass a request through a chain of handlers. Each handler decides to process it or pass it on."
Simple Analogy
A loan application goes through multiple desks: Clerk → Manager → Senior Manager → Director. Each person either approves or passes it to the next level.
// Handler interface
abstract class LoanApprovalHandler {
    protected LoanApprovalHandler next;

    public LoanApprovalHandler setNext(LoanApprovalHandler next) {
        this.next = next;
        return next;
    }

    public abstract void approve(double loanAmount);
}

// Each handler handles its own range
class ClerkHandler extends LoanApprovalHandler {
    public void approve(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 50000) {
            System.out.println("Clerk approved ₹" + amount); // handles it
        } else if (next != null) {
            next.approve(amount); // passes to next
        }
    }
}

class ManagerHandler extends LoanApprovalHandler {
    public void approve(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 500000) {
            System.out.println("Manager approved ₹" + amount);
        } else if (next != null) {
            next.approve(amount);
        }
    }
}

class DirectorHandler extends LoanApprovalHandler {
    public void approve(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 5000000) {
            System.out.println("Director approved ₹" + amount);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Amount too large — rejected ₹" + amount);
        }
    }
}

// Build the chain
LoanApprovalHandler clerk = new ClerkHandler();
LoanApprovalHandler manager = new ManagerHandler();
LoanApprovalHandler director = new DirectorHandler();

clerk.setNext(manager).setNext(director); // chain built ✅

// Test
clerk.approve(30000);    // "Clerk approved ₹30000"
clerk.approve(200000);   // "Manager approved ₹200000"
clerk.approve(2000000);  // "Director approved ₹2000000"
clerk.approve(9999999);  // "Amount too large — rejected"
🌱 Spring Boot — Security Filter Chain IS Chain of Responsibility!
// Spring Security internally uses Chain of Responsibility!
// Every HTTP request passes through a chain of filters:

UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter  // checks username/password
    ↓ (if passes)
BasicAuthenticationFilter             // checks Basic auth header
    ↓ (if passes)
JwtAuthenticationFilter               // checks JWT token
    ↓ (if passes)
AuthorizationFilter                   // checks permissions
    ↓ (if passes)
Your Controller                       // finally reaches your code ✅

// If ANY filter rejects → request stopped, 401/403 returned
// Your controller never even knows about failed requests ✅
Interview Line
"Spring Security's filter chain is Chain of Responsibility. Each filter either processes the request or passes it on. JWT filter validates token — if valid, passes to authorization filter — if authorized, reaches controller. If any filter rejects — chain stops, user gets 401."

🗺️ Complete Cheat Sheet
When to Use What
Creating objects?
├── Need ONE instance?              → Singleton
├── Centralise creation logic?      → Factory
└── Complex object, many fields?    → Builder

Connecting objects?
├── Add logging/security/caching?   → Proxy
├── Old API + New system?           → Adapter
└── Stack extra features on top?    → Decorator

Object communication?
├── Swap algorithms at runtime?     → Strategy
├── Notify multiple objects?        → Observer
├── Fixed steps, variable parts?    → Template Method
└── Pass through approval chain?    → Chain of Responsibility

